## Rules for every agent

1. Never change files in `System/` without showing the user the exact change and getting a yes (the only exception: saving the user's own name and company to `System/Settings.md` when they tell you).
2. Never edit `System/Core/`, `.claude/`, `.codex/`, `.agents/`, `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md`. Bron regenerates them from `System/`.
3. Team members never run as subagents. To hand work to a team member, use a ticket. Subagents are only for helpers.
4. Keep the vault tidy: one-off work in `Projects/`, repeating work in `Routines/`. Machine data lives in `.bron/`.
5. Ask before anything that leaves the vault (sending, sharing, posting, pushing) unless the user chose "always allow" for it.

## Tickets

Work between agents goes through tickets in `Tickets/` (see `System/Core/Manual/tickets.md`).
- Hand work to a team member only when it needs their connections, their model or work they own (their projects and routines). Anything else, do yourself.
- Quick question or short task for a team member: run `.bron/bin/bron ticket new --to <Agent> --from <your name> --title '<short title>' --request '<what to do>' [--context '<what they need to know>'] --run --caller-cli <claude|codex>` as an ordinary command and wait (it takes about 10–20 seconds). When you pass on the user's question, use their own words: don't add tasks, sources or checks they didn't ask for. When it prints the Result, give the user the answer first, then close it with `.bron/bin/bron ticket status <id> done --as <your key>`. For longer work, questions back, approvals or anything that doesn't go to plan, use the delegate skill.
- When the message trigger says a tagged agent (`@name`) is answering, don't answer for them: wait with `.bron/bin/bron ticket wait <id>` and show their reply word for word.
- When you were started to work a ticket, your final reply becomes its Result; just answer. To stop and ask a question, run `.bron/bin/bron ticket status <id> blocked --as <your key> --note '<question>'` (if the text contains a single quote, write it to a file and use `--file` (result) or `--note-file` (status)). Your ticket id is in the task you were given (also in $$BRON_TICKET); your key is your agent name in lower case with hyphens (e.g. cfo).
- If an action is refused or needs approval, don't work around it: mark the ticket blocked with a note starting "Needs your OK:" that names the exact action.

